WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.866
This is AWS Public Change Alerting.

2
00:00:02.866 --> 00:00:12.179
I built it to turn public AWS announcements into a
short, explainable review feed for the teams that use
the affected services.

3
00:00:12.179 --> 00:00:17.825
This is the dev MVP evidence pack, captured after the
first milestone closed.

4
00:00:18.675 --> 00:00:20.142
Here’s the whole trip.

5
00:00:20.142 --> 00:00:27.945
A watcher reads approved RSS and Atom feeds, saves the
raw response, and works out which service and risk rule
fired.

6
00:00:27.945 --> 00:00:32.947
The candidate is tied to configured environments, then
written to DynamoDB.

7
00:00:32.947 --> 00:00:39.350
SQS carries the delivery request to Slack, and the
result comes back to the same durable record.

8
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:43.138
Under that path are four small Lambda jobs.

9
00:00:43.138 --> 00:00:45.324
The watcher gathers and matches.

10
00:00:45.324 --> 00:00:47.442
The dispatcher claims due work.

11
00:00:47.442 --> 00:00:50.175
The Slack worker makes the network call.

12
00:00:50.175 --> 00:00:54.548
The reconciler picks up work that got stuck or became
uncertain.

13
00:00:54.548 --> 00:01:00.150
They share one exact package, while DynamoDB owns the
state and SQS owns ordering.

14
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.192
I kept the infrastructure in three Terraform roots, each
with a different job.

15
00:01:06.192 --> 00:01:09.720
Bootstrap owns remote state and its locking boundary.

16
00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:12.383
Central owns the persistent dev service.

17
00:01:12.383 --> 00:01:22.700
Preflight spins up an isolated copy for recovery and
load exercises, points it at the same immutable package,
then tears down against a saved destroy plan.

18
00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:27.335
Delivery only matters if the matcher is worth listening
to.

19
00:01:27.335 --> 00:01:33.879
The current corpus has forty-seven labeled
announcements: thirty-two historical and fifteen
synthetic.

20
00:01:33.879 --> 00:01:41.450
This run produced twenty-nine true-positive service and
risk pairs with zero false positives and zero false
negatives.

21
00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:42.926
The global gate passes.

22
00:01:42.926 --> 00:01:46.775
A few pairs are still thin, so I’ve kept that limit
visible.

23
00:01:47.625 --> 00:01:49.587
This is one candidate in full.

24
00:01:49.587 --> 00:01:59.201
You can see the original announcement, the exact service
alias and phrase that matched, the route, and the three
environments that may need review.

25
00:01:59.201 --> 00:02:04.825
The candidate ID commits those inputs, which makes a
replay land on the same decision.

26
00:02:05.675 --> 00:02:08.726
That identity stays with the delivery request.

27
00:02:08.726 --> 00:02:11.844
The dispatcher moves it from pending to queued.

28
00:02:11.844 --> 00:02:16.619
The worker claims a lease, makes the Slack call, and
writes the outcome.

29
00:02:16.619 --> 00:02:19.007
A documented success becomes posted.

30
00:02:19.007 --> 00:02:23.650
An ambiguous result stays delivery unknown for an
operator to examine.

31
00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:29.275
The panel on the left is the message generated by the
real renderer.

32
00:02:29.275 --> 00:02:38.615
The live exercise is recorded separately: twelve posts
reached Slack, each with one network attempt and an HTTP
two hundred response.

33
00:02:38.615 --> 00:02:41.775
DynamoDB recorded posted for the same cohort.

34
00:02:42.625 --> 00:02:44.758
So, what closed the milestone?

35
00:02:44.758 --> 00:02:47.246
Four runtime triggers were enabled.

36
00:02:47.246 --> 00:02:50.018
Twelve public-feed posts reached Slack.

37
00:02:50.018 --> 00:02:51.938
Both recovery cases passed.

38
00:02:51.938 --> 00:02:58.549
The isolated load run created fifty messages at five per
minute, and the alarm email arrived.

39
00:02:58.549 --> 00:03:01.606
That gives the dev MVP executable evidence.

40
00:03:01.606 --> 00:03:04.450
Production readiness stays a later gate.
